+# Native timing consumer probe
+
+Opt-in execution through the existing public ChainBench composition owner and
+`timing_investigation.py`. Requires a clean source-pinned ChainBench CLI and Go
+1.26.9. Use a new output directory:
+
+```sh
+python3 bench/timing/probe.py --chainbench "$CHAINBENCH_BIN" --output /tmp/new-timing-evidence
+```
+
+The probe builds and bundles two actual clean Git revisions of an owned Go RPC
+program. The first reads before a concurrent commit; the second waits for that
+commit. It executes60 reproduction trials and60 paired before/after trials,
+normal controls, explicit delays, native report verification, consumer bindings
+and owned cleanup. The source accepts the Stablenet launch/config shape solely
+as a controlled protocol fixture. It is not blockchain consensus or production
+validity. Actual go-stablenet four-validator composition is tested separately.
+
+Evidence includes the fixture Git bundle, actual binary source metadata,
+immutable phase plans, stdout/stderr and native reports under the ticket
+workspace. `oracle-validity-report.json` at the end contains only the actual
+source coordinates and NOT_EXECUTED, never a fabricated oracle PASS. The full
+oracle and candidate-selection gates are still required for ticket completion.
+
+The fault controls reuse only the probe's own clean fixture binaries:
+
+```sh
+python3 bench/timing/faults.py --chainbench "$CHAINBENCH_BIN" --fixture /tmp/timing-evidence --output /tmp/new-fault-evidence
+```
+
+They exercise actual restart, refused peer injection, DSL timeout, missing
+trial denominators, one graceful cancellation, killed-consumer parent watching,
+and cleanup refusal when an owned launch record disagrees with the actual
+process. Cleanup refusal must stop further trials. The probe restores only its
+own deliberately modified record, invokes ownership-verified recovery stop,
+and observes each owned node stopped/missing. A recovered cleanup failure stays
+a recorded failure; it is not rewritten to successful native cleanup.
